{"type":"entity","name":"Recall_Tool","entityType":"SystemConfig","observations":["A searchable store of questions already answered, with the raw evidence behind each answer. Project at ~/recall-mcp (git repo); runtime at ~/.shared_memory/; store at ~/.shared_memory/recall.db.","TERMINAL USE (zero tokens): `recall search \"caching gain\"`. Symlinked into ~/.local/bin so it works from any directory. Also: recall add / show / supersede / stats, and `recall --help`.","IN A CLI: no slash command. It is an MCP tool (recall_search, recall_add, recall_get, recall_supersede) whose description tells the model to check before re-deriving. Nudge with \"check recall first\". Registered in ~/.claude.json, ~/.codex/config.toml and ~/.gemini/settings.json, all pointing at python3 ~/.shared_memory/recall_mcp.py against ONE shared SQLite file - an entry written by one CLI is visible to all.","WHY: on 2026-08-13 'how much did caching gain?' was answered by re-running the benchmark instead of reading recorded numbers. The re-run was mis-constructed (cold connect vs warm pool), gave 0.5x then 146x, neither matching the recorded 1.27s -> 0.01s. Measured cost: ~9,400 tokens re-deriving versus ~217 to look up, and the lookup is the correct answer.","NO VECTOR DB, deliberately. ~1k entries / ~1 MB; SQLite FTS5 is stdlib, needs no embedding model on each client, costs no tokens per query, works offline. Measured 0.74 ms/search."]}
